{"id": "civic_legaltaxin_06fa144c_01", "source_url": "https://legaltax.in/blogs/government-website-development-guidelines/", "title": "Government Website Development Guidelines In India: What Businesses And Agencies Must Know | LegalTax", "section": "Overview", "text": "Views: 14 Introduction Why Government Website Development Guidelines in India Are Non-Negotiable for Every Agency and Business Working with Public Sector Clients The digital transformation of India’s government services has been one of the most ambitious and far-reaching technology initiatives in the country’s history. From the National e-Governance Plan to the Digital India programme, from the UMANG app to the India Stack infrastructure, the Indian government has invested enormously in building digital platforms that serve hundreds of millions of citizens across every state and territory. Behind every government website, every e-governance portal, and every public service digital interface is a development team, an agency, or a technology business that built and maintains that digital asset. If your business or agency develops websites, web applications, or digital portals for government clients at the central, state, or local level, understanding Government Website Development Guidelines in India is not optional knowledge. It is a fundamental requirement of doing your job correctly, delivering compliant work to your clients, and protecting both your business and your government clients from the legal, security, and accessibility failures that result from non-compliant digital development. The Government Website Development Guidelines in India are comprehensive, detailed, and technically demanding. They cover everything from the technology stack and hosting requirements to accessibility standards, security protocols, content management rules, domain naming conventions, and legal compliance obligations. Non-compliance with these guidelines can result in rejection of delivered work, financial penalties in government contracts, reputational damage, and in serious cases involving security failures, legal liability. This complete guide covers all the key dimensions of Government Website Development Guidelines in India that every web development agency, technology company, and freelance developer working with government clients must understand and implement. It covers the official guidelines framework, the technical requirements, the accessibility mandates, the security obligations, the hosting and domain requirements, the legal and IP considerations, and the compliance verification process. For all legal compliance, intellectual property protection, and business legal matters related to your technology business working with government clients, visit LegalIP.in or call our team at +91 9711939395. The Official Framework: Guidelines for Indian Government Websites and Applications What Are the GIGW Guidelines? The primary official framework governing Government Website Development Guidelines in India is the Guidelines for Indian Government Websites and Applications, commonly referred to as the GIGW guidelines. These guidelines are issued and periodically updated by the National Informatics Centre under the Ministry of Electronics and Information Technology, which is the primary government technology agency responsible for setting digital standards for government services in India. The GIGW guidelines were first issued in 2009 and have been updated multiple times to reflect evolving technology standards, security requirements, accessibility obligations, and the growing importance of mobile-first design. The current version of the GIGW guidelines covers a comprehensive range of requirements that all government websites and web applications must meet, regardless of the government body they", "category": "civic", "last_verified": "2026-10-25", "status": "verified", "quality_score": 0.92, "source_confidence": "official", "verification_method": "automated_official_source", "retrieval_tags": ["acts", "citizen", "guideline", "info", "legaltaxin", "portal", "process", "rules", "service"], "language": "en"}
{"id": "civic_legaltaxin_f02db7dd_01", "source_url": "https://legaltax.in/trademark-registration", "title": "legaltax.in - Trademark Services", "section": "Overview", "text": "Trademark Registration Online in Delhi At LegalTax, Delhi's trusted legal partner, we specialize in simplifying complex legal, tax, and compliance processes for businesses of all sizes. Whether you're looking for company registration, trademark protection, tax filings, or ISO certifications, we provide expert services that ensure your business is fully compliant with Indian regulations. Our dedicated team of professionals in Delhi guides you at every step, offering tailored solutions to meet your unique needs. With LegalTax, you can focus on growing your business while we take care of all your legal and financial obligations, ensuring peace of mind and long-term success. Get your Trademark Today Our Application Process for Trademark Registration in Delhi Overview of Online Trademark Registration in Delhi Trademark registration online is a vital legal process that provides businesses with exclusive rights to protect their brand names, logos, slogans, and other distinctive marks. Registering a trademark helps in establishing a unique identity for products or services, prevents unauthorized use by competitors, and allows the trademark owner to take legal action against infringement. With the advent of technology, the process of TM registration online has become more streamlined and accessible for businesses in Delhi and across India. The trademark registration process for businesses in Delhi can be completed online through the Trademark Registry portal, which is operated by the Controller General of Patents, Designs, and Trademarks (CGPDTM) under the Ministry of Commerce and Industry's Department for Promotion of Industry and Internal Trade (DPIIT). What is the Importance of Trademark Registration in Delhi? Trademark registration in Delhi holds significant importance for businesses and individuals alike. Here are some key reasons why it is essential: - Legal Protection: Registering a trademark provides legal protection to the owner by granting exclusive rights to use the mark for the goods or services it represents. It helps prevent others from using a similar or identical mark, reducing the risk of infringement. - Brand Identity: Trademarks play a crucial role in establishing and maintaining a brand's identity. It allows consumers to recognize and distinguish products or services associated with a particular business, leading to brand loyalty and goodwill. - Business Reputation: A registered trademark enhances the reputation and credibility of a business. It signifies that the mark has met the required standards and has been officially recognized by the government. - National Protection: Registration provides protection across India, including strong enforcement in Delhi's business hubs. It prevents others from using the registered mark for similar goods or services, strengthening the position of the trademark owner. - Exclusive Use: Once registered, the owner gains the exclusive right to use the mark for the goods or services it represents. This helps in maintaining a competitive advantage and prevents others from riding on the brand's success. - Legal Remedies: Registration provides a legal basis for taking action against infringers. In case of any unauthorized use, the trademark owner can", "category": "civic", "last_verified": "2026-10-25", "status": "verified", "quality_score": 0.92, "source_confidence": "official", "verification_method": "automated_official_source", "retrieval_tags": ["help", "legaltaxin", "overview", "portal", "process", "registration", "service"], "language": "en"}
{"id": "civic_legaltaxin_ff45eddd_01", "source_url": "https://legaltax.in/blogs/about-us/", "title": "legaltax.in - About", "section": "Overview", "text": "Views: 32 About LegalTax.in We didn’t start in a boardroom. We started with one person, one laptop, and a problem nobody was solving simply enough. In 2018, Subhash Chandra Bose started LegalTax alone. No co-founders. No investors. No safety net. Just a stubborn belief that legal, tax, and compliance services in India were broken — too complicated, too expensive, and too inaccessible for the people who needed them most. Startups were drowning in paperwork before they even had a product. Founders were paying lakhs for basic compliance they didn’t understand. And everyday people had no idea where to even begin. So he built the solution he wished existed. One client at a time. One problem at a time. Today, LegalTax is a full-stack legal and business services platform — helping startups, entrepreneurs, and growing businesses navigate law, tax, compliance, and digital growth — all under one roof. Our Mission To make legal and financial services simple, affordable, and accessible — for every founder, every startup, every dreamer building something real. No jargon. No unnecessary complexity. No gatekeeping. Just clear guidance, honest advice, and execution that actually gets done. Our Vision A future where no startup fails because of legal or compliance gaps. We believe the best ideas deserve the best foundation. Whether you’re registering your first company, filing your first GST return, protecting your first trademark, or scaling your digital presence — LegalTax is built to be the partner that grows with you. From day one to IPO. The Founder Subhash Chandra Bose — Founder, LegalTax There’s a certain kind of person who sees a broken system and decides to fix it instead of complaining about it. Mr. Subhash is that person. In 2018, with no backing, no team, and no guarantees — he started LegalTax from scratch. What kept him going wasn’t funding or fanfare. It was founders reaching out at midnight confused about their company structure. It was small business owners who’d been overcharged and underserved for years. It was the quiet satisfaction of making something complicated — finally feel simple. He built LegalTax the hard way. Which is also the right way. Every system, every service, every process at LegalTax carries that DNA — built by someone who knows what it means to figure things out alone, and decided nobody else should have to. What We Do 1. Legal Services — Divorce, matrimonial disputes, child custody, documentation, and more 2. Company Incorporation & CA Services — Private limited, LLP, OPC, GST, ITR, compliance 3. Trademark & IP Protection — Trademark filing, objections, copyright, patent guidance 4. Digital Marketing — SEO, content, social media, and growth for startups and businesses", "category": "civic", "last_verified": "2026-10-25", "status": "verified", "quality_score": 0.92, "source_confidence": "official", "verification_method": "automated_official_source", "retrieval_tags": ["about", "help", "legaltaxin", "process", "service"], "language": "en"}
{"id": "civic_legaltaxin_a62dc322_01", "source_url": "https://legaltax.in/private-limited-company", "title": "legaltax.in - Services", "section": "Overview", "text": "Private Limited Company Registration Online in India Planning to start a Private Limited Company in India? LegalTax provides complete online assistance for company registration, ensuring a smooth and legally compliant incorporation process. Our experts guide you through name approval, document preparation, and filing with the Ministry of Corporate Affairs (MCA) quickly and efficiently. At LegalTax, we handle DSC and DIN application, e-MOA and e-AOA drafting, and SPICe+ incorporation filing to secure your Certificate of Incorporation along with PAN and TAN. With our professional online service, you can benefit from limited liability, a separate legal identity, and enhanced business credibility across India. Contact Us Today What is a Private Limited Company? A Private Limited Company is a separate legal entity registered under the Companies Act, 2013. It is owned by a minimum of two shareholders, managed by a minimum of two directors, and offers limited liability, brand recognition and perpetual succession. It is the most preferred business structure for startups and growing businesses in India because it makes raising funds from investors and banks easier. What is Company Registration in India? Company registration is the process of incorporating your business with the Registrar of Companies (ROC) under the Ministry of Corporate Affairs (MCA). Once registered, the company gets a Certificate of Incorporation, a Corporate Identification Number (CIN), PAN and TAN, and exists as a legal entity separate from its owners. A registered company can open a current account in its own name, sign contracts, own property, take loans and apply for government schemes and licences such as GST, MSME and Startup India. Benefits of Private Limited Company Registration - Limited liability: Shareholders are liable only up to the unpaid value of their shares, so personal assets stay protected. - Separate legal entity: The company can own assets, sue and be sued in its own name. - Easy fundraising: Equity can be issued to investors, angel funds and venture capital firms. - Perpetual succession: The company continues even if a director or shareholder leaves or passes away. - Credibility: Customers, banks and vendors trust a registered company more than an unregistered business. - Tax benefits: Eligible companies can opt for concessional tax regimes and claim business expenses as per the Income Tax Act. - Brand protection: Your company name is protected under company law, and you can further secure it with trademark registration. Why Should You Register Your Company? - Raise capital from investors and banks - Build goodwill and customer trust - Protect promoters through limited liability - Ensure perpetual existence of the business - Hire employees, sign large contracts and bid for tenders - Qualify for government schemes, Startup India and MSME benefits Eligibility and Rules for Private Limited Company Registration - A minimum of 2 directors and 2 shareholders is required. The same person can be both a director and a shareholder. - The maximum number of members is", "category": "civic", "last_verified": "2026-10-25", "status": "verified", "quality_score": 0.92, "source_confidence": "official", "verification_method": "automated_official_source", "retrieval_tags": ["acts", "apply", "contact", "eligibility", "legaltaxin", "process", "registration", "rules", "scheme", "service"], "language": "en"}
{"id": "civic_legaltaxin_ba543a2c_01", "source_url": "https://legaltax.in/contact", "title": "Contact LegalTax.in | Legal & Tax Experts in Delhi", "section": "Overview", "text": "Get in Touch with LegalTax Delhi Simply fill in the below details and our Delhi-based team will contact you soon!! - Address: PLOT NO 454, 2ND FLOOR, KAKROLA HOUSING COMPLEX, DWARKA MOR, OPP METRO PILLAR NO 795 NEW DELHI-110078. - Phone: +91 9711939395 - Phone: +91 8750939395 - Email: info@legaltax.in - Website: https://legaltax.in/", "category": "civic", "last_verified": "2026-10-25", "status": "verified", "quality_score": 0.92, "source_confidence": "official", "verification_method": "automated_official_source", "retrieval_tags": ["contact", "info", "legaltaxin"], "language": "en"}
{"id": "civic_guidelinesindiagovin_6b0447b2_01", "source_url": "https://guidelines.india.gov.in/guidelines/", "title": "Guidelines | Guidelines for Indian Government Websites and apps (GIGW) | India", "section": "Overview", "text": "GIGW 3.0 - Guidelines - Quality: Guidelines and attributes - Statement: Association to government is demonstrated by the use of emblem/logo in proper ratio and colour, prominently displayed on the homepage of the website or homescreen of the app. Benefit:Reassures visitors that the government website/app is authentic and trustworthy. Moreover, the government organisation’s brand identity is reinforced. Government organisation action:Government organisations must select proper visual identity elements like emblems or logos to highlight government ownership of the website/app. The State emblem of India must be displayed on the homepages of the websites and homescreens of the apps of Central Government Ministries/Departments. State Governments should display their Emblems and public Sector organisations and autonomous bodies should display their official logo on the homepage of the website or homescreen of the app to reinforce their identity Developer Action: Visual Identity elements (logos and emblem) must prominently stand out on the page, in a proper ratio and colour. Developers must ensure that all logo images are accompanied by proper alternate text so that the screen reader users may be informed of the same. The usage of the State Emblem of India must comply with the provisions of the State Emblem of India (Prohibition of Improper Use) Act, 2005. Evaluator Action: The evaluator shall manually test the website/app for verifying conformity with this. - Statement: Ownership information is displayed on the homepage/homescreen and on all important entry pages/screens of the website/app and each subsequent page/screen is a standalone entity in terms of ownership, navigation and context of content. Benefit: Helps to build trust in the visitors and reinforces the government organisation’s branding. Also, search engines use the ownership information to verify the legitimacy of the websites/apps and accord higher ranks in search results. Government organisation action: Provide complete ownership information for publishing on the website Developer Action:Ensure display of ownership information, either in the header or footer, on the homepage/homescreen and all-important entry pages/screens of the website/app. Evaluator Action: The evaluator shall manually test the website/app for verifying conformity with this. - Statement: Source of all documents, not owned by the dept. that have been reproduced in part or full, is mentioned. Benefit: Audiences can rely upon the credibility of authentic information being published by the government organisation. Additionally, giving the credit to the authoring organisation helps establish an environment of respect for intellectual property of other organisations and discourages plagiarism. Government organisation action:If any published government document/report is being reproduced on the government organisation’s website/app, whether as excerpts or in full, the organisation must cite the source of the same (complete title of the document/report, along with the name of the owner organisation and the year of publication). Developer Action: Ensure provision of appropriate data input fields in the CMS to input the source (owner organisation of each published document/report and the year of publication). Evaluator action :The evaluator shall manually test the", "category": "civic", "last_verified": "2026-10-25", "status": "verified", "quality_score": 0.92, "source_confidence": "official", "verification_method": "automated_official_source", "retrieval_tags": ["documents", "guideline", "guidelinesindiagovin", "help", "info", "manual", "verify"], "language": "en"}
{"id": "civic_wwwuxdtnicin_08ea2ca2_01", "source_url": "https://www.uxdt.nic.in/guidelines/technical-considerations/gigw-guidelines-for-indian-government-websites/", "title": "UI/UX Guidelines - User Experience Design & Technology", "section": "Overview", "text": "The Guidelines for Indian Government Websites (GIGW) are a comprehensive set of standards and best practices established by the National Informatics Centre (NIC) to ensure that government websites and portals in India are user-friendly, accessible, secure, and reliable. These guidelines help maintain uniformity, transparency, and accountability across digital platforms managed by government entities. What Are GIGW? GIGW provides a standardized framework for designing, developing, and maintaining government websites and portals in India. These guidelines address technical, design, content, and operational aspects to ensure platforms meet user needs while adhering to national and international standards. Why Are GIGW Important? User Experience: Improves usability and navigation for diverse user groups, including citizens, businesses, and government officials. Accessibility: Ensures platforms are inclusive for individuals with disabilities, following principles like those in WCAG. Security: Safeguards sensitive government data and user information. Uniformity: Promotes consistency across various government platforms. Transparency: Builds trust through clear, reliable, and up-to-date information. Key Features of GIGW Accessibility Adhere to WCAG standards to ensure inclusivity for users with disabilities. Provide features like screen reader compatibility, high-contrast themes, and keyboard navigation. Example: Ensure all images have descriptive alt text for visually impaired users. Usability Design intuitive and user-friendly interfaces with simple navigation. Use responsive designs to ensure compatibility across mobile devices, tablets, and desktops. Example: Create a clean homepage layout with a prominent search bar and clearly labeled sections. Security Implement HTTPS with SSL/TLS encryption to secure communication. Use multi-factor authentication for sensitive administrative functions. Example: Display a security badge and padlock icon in the browser to indicate a secure connection. Content Management Ensure content is accurate, up-to-date, and relevant. Provide multilingual support to cater to India’s linguistic diversity. Use a standardized format for presenting information, such as FAQs, forms, and policies. Example: Publish content in both Hindi and English and provide regional language options. Performance Optimization Optimize websites for faster load times by compressing images, minimizing scripts, and using Content Delivery Networks (CDNs). Conduct regular performance testing to ensure smooth operation during peak usage. Example: A government portal should load within 3 seconds, even on slower internet connections. Scalability Design platforms to handle an increasing number of users and growing data volumes. Use cloud-based or modular architectures for flexibility. Example: Ensure an e-governance portal remains functional during a high-traffic event like tax filing deadlines. Legal and Regulatory Compliance Ensure adherence to laws like the IT Act, 2000 and other national standards. Meet data protection and privacy requirements under India’s evolving data protection laws. Mandatory Guidelines Under GIGW GIGW outlines several mandatory requirements for Indian government websites: Domain Name: Use official domain extensions like .gov.in or .nic.in to signify authenticity. Example: ruraldevelopment.gov.in. Content Ownership: Clearly specify the content owner and update frequency for all sections. Example: “Last updated on [Date]” on every page. Feedback Mechanism: Include a user feedback form or grievance redressal mechanism. Example: Provide a “Feedback” or “Contact Us”", "category": "civic", "last_verified": "2026-10-25", "status": "verified", "quality_score": 0.92, "source_confidence": "official", "verification_method": "automated_official_source", "retrieval_tags": ["citizen", "contact", "faq", "grievance", "guideline", "help", "info", "portal", "wwwuxdtnicin"], "language": "en"}
{"id": "civic_wwwuxdtnicin_0286dfff_01", "source_url": "https://www.uxdt.nic.in/guidelines/the-ux-design-process/setting-the-foundation/", "title": "www.uxdt.nic.in - Setting the Foundation", "section": "Overview", "text": "The UX design process is a systematic approach to creating user-centric platforms that are functional, accessible, and enjoyable. It involves understanding user needs, designing solutions, testing prototypes, and refining the platform to deliver optimal experiences. By following a structured process, teams can ensure that government digital platforms meet the diverse expectations of users. What is the UX Design Process? The UX design process is a series of steps that guide the creation of a platform from initial research to final implementation and ongoing improvement. It is iterative, meaning that feedback and insights are continually incorporated to refine the design. Stages of the UX Design Process 1. Define Objectives - Clearly identify the goals of the project and the problems it aims to solve. - Align objectives with user needs and organizational priorities. Key Activities: - Stakeholder meetings to understand project requirements. - Documentation of objectives, scope, and success criteria. 2. Conduct User Research Understanding the target audience is critical to creating user-centered designs. This stage focuses on gathering insights into user behaviors, needs, and pain points. Key Activities: - User Interviews: Engage with actual users to understand their challenges and preferences. - Surveys: Collect data from a broader audience to identify trends. - Journey Mapping: Visualize the steps users take to achieve their goals. - Persona Development: Create detailed profiles representing different user groups. 3. Ideation and Conceptualization This stage involves brainstorming solutions based on the insights gathered during research. The goal is to generate ideas that address user needs effectively. Key Activities: - Sketching and Brainstorming: Collaborate with teams to outline potential solutions. - Wireframing: Create low-fidelity layouts to map out basic workflows and structure. - Storyboarding: Illustrate how users will interact with the platform in different scenarios. 4. Prototyping Prototyping involves creating interactive models of the platform to test its functionality and design before full development. Key Activities: - Low-Fidelity Prototypes: Focus on basic interactions and layouts without detailed design elements. - High-Fidelity Prototypes: Develop detailed, visually appealing models that closely resemble the final product. - Interactive Mockups: Enable users to interact with the prototype to simulate real-world usage. 5. Usability Testing Testing prototypes with real users ensures the platform meets their needs and identifies areas for improvement. Key Activities: - Task-Based Testing: Observe how users perform specific tasks on the platform. - Feedback Collection: Gather user opinions on the design and functionality. - Iterative Improvements: Refine the design based on testing insights. 6. Implementation Once the design is finalized, it is handed over to developers for coding and integration into the platform. Key Activities: - Collaboration Between Designers and Developers: Ensure the final product aligns with design specifications. - Quality Assurance Testing: Test for bugs, responsiveness, and performance. - Accessibility Compliance Checks: Verify adherence to standards like WCAG. 7. Post-Launch Evaluation After the platform is live, monitoring its performance and user feedback is critical for continuous improvement.", "category": "civic", "last_verified": "2026-10-25", "status": "verified", "quality_score": 0.92, "source_confidence": "official", "verification_method": "automated_official_source", "retrieval_tags": ["process", "verify", "wwwuxdtnicin"], "language": "en"}
{"id": "civic_wwwuxdtnicin_b654af67_01", "source_url": "https://www.uxdt.nic.in/guidelines/the-ux-design-process/defining-objectives/", "title": "www.uxdt.nic.in - Defining Objectives", "section": "Overview", "text": "Defining clear objectives is the first and most critical step in the UX design process. It sets the direction for the project, aligns stakeholders on shared goals, and ensures the final platform meets user needs while achieving organizational outcomes. Objectives act as the foundation upon which all subsequent design decisions are made. What Does Defining Objectives Mean? Defining objectives involves identifying what the platform aims to accomplish and specifying the problems it intends to solve. It bridges the gap between user expectations and organizational requirements, creating a shared vision for the project. Key Steps in Defining Objectives 1. Understand the Project Scope - Clearly outline what the project will deliver and what it will not. - Define the target audience, including their needs, behaviors, and pain points. - Specify the platform type (e.g., mobile app, web portal) and its intended functionalities. Example: A subsidy application portal’s scope may include multilingual support, offline capabilities, and secure document uploads. 2. Align Stakeholders - Involve decision-makers, designers, developers, and other relevant teams to gather input. - Ensure alignment between user needs and organizational priorities. Example: A tax-filing portal’s objectives might balance user-friendly workflows with compliance requirements for accurate data collection. 3. Define Success Metrics - Establish measurable criteria to evaluate the platform’s success. - Focus on metrics such as task completion rates, user satisfaction scores, error reduction, and system performance. Example: For a grievance redressal platform, success could be measured by reduced complaint resolution time and high user satisfaction ratings. 4. Identify User Goals - Specify what tasks users should accomplish through the platform. - Highlight user pain points that the platform will address. Example: A job application portal should help users easily search for jobs, upload resumes, and track application statuses. 5. Prioritize Features - Determine which features are essential for the platform’s launch and which can be added later. - Use methods like MoSCoW (Must-have, Should-have, Could-have, Won’t-have) prioritization to organize features. Example: For a health services app, essential features might include appointment booking and test results, while community forums could be added in future updates. 6. Consider Accessibility and Inclusivity - Ensure the platform is designed for diverse users, including those with disabilities or limited digital literacy. - Include objectives that align with accessibility standards such as WCAG. Example: A public education website could prioritize objectives like multilingual content and screen reader compatibility. Best Practices for Defining Objectives 1. Be Specific and Realistic - Avoid vague objectives like “improve user experience.” Instead, specify, “reduce average task completion time by 20%.” 2. Document Objectives Clearly - Use clear, concise language to describe objectives, ensuring all stakeholders have a shared understanding. 3. Validate with Users - Conduct user research to ensure objectives align with real user needs and expectations. 4. Keep Objectives Flexible - Allow room for iteration and adjustment based on user feedback and testing outcomes. Examples of Well-Defined Objectives -", "category": "civic", "last_verified": "2026-10-25", "status": "verified", "quality_score": 0.92, "source_confidence": "official", "verification_method": "automated_official_source", "retrieval_tags": ["grievance", "help", "portal", "process", "service", "status", "track", "wwwuxdtnicin"], "language": "en"}
{"id": "civic_wwwuxdtnicin_6ab98576_01", "source_url": "https://www.uxdt.nic.in/guidelines/the-ux-design-process/identifying-key-stakeholders/", "title": "www.uxdt.nic.in - Identifying Key Stakeholders", "section": "Overview", "text": "Identifying key stakeholders is a crucial step in the UX design process. Stakeholders are individuals or groups with a vested interest in the project, including those who influence decisions, develop and implement the platform, or rely on its success. Engaging the right stakeholders early ensures alignment, clarity, and collaboration throughout the project lifecycle. Who are Stakeholders? Stakeholders can be categorized into two broad groups: - Internal Stakeholders: Teams or individuals directly involved in planning, designing, and implementing the platform. - External Stakeholders: End users, regulatory authorities, or partners impacted by the platform’s outcomes. Key Stakeholders in the UX Design Process 1. Decision Makers - Who They Are: Senior officials, project managers, and organizational leaders responsible for approving strategies and allocating resources. - Role: Define project goals, approve budgets, and ensure alignment with organizational objectives. - How to Engage Them: - Present clear goals, timelines, and measurable outcomes. - Regularly update them on project progress and key milestones. 2. UX Designers - Who They Are: Professionals responsible for designing the platform’s interface and user experience. - Role: Translate user needs into intuitive designs, wireframes, and prototypes. - How to Engage Them: - Involve them early to align design decisions with user research findings. - Provide access to user data, personas, and feedback. 3. Developers - Who They Are: Front-end, back-end, and full-stack developers responsible for coding and technical implementation. - Role: Turn designs into functional platforms and ensure technical feasibility. - How to Engage Them: - Collaborate during design handoff to clarify technical requirements. - Ensure they understand usability and accessibility standards. 4. End Users - Who They Are: The individuals who will use the platform, such as citizens, employees, or service providers. - Role: Provide insights into needs, expectations, and pain points. - How to Engage Them: - Conduct interviews, surveys, and usability testing. - Create personas and journey maps based on their input. 5. Content Creators - Who They Are: Teams responsible for creating written, visual, or multimedia content for the platform. - Role: Develop clear, accessible, and user-friendly content. - How to Engage Them: - Provide style guides and content requirements. - Collaborate to ensure content aligns with user goals and accessibility standards. 6. Accessibility Experts - Who They Are: Specialists in designing platforms for users with disabilities or diverse needs. - Role: Ensure the platform complies with accessibility standards like WCAG. - How to Engage Them: - Involve them during the design and testing phases. - Use their expertise to audit the platform for accessibility compliance. 7. Regulatory Authorities - Who They Are: Government or industry bodies that set compliance standards and guidelines. - Role: Ensure the platform meets legal and policy requirements. - How to Engage Them: - Share project documentation for review and approval. - Stay updated on changes in regulatory standards. 8. Data Analysts - Who They Are: Professionals who interpret analytics and user", "category": "civic", "last_verified": "2026-10-25", "status": "verified", "quality_score": 0.92, "source_confidence": "official", "verification_method": "automated_official_source", "retrieval_tags": ["citizen", "guideline", "process", "service", "wwwuxdtnicin"], "language": "en"}
{"id": "civic_wwwuxdtnicin_0cef75c1_01", "source_url": "https://www.uxdt.nic.in/guidelines/the-ux-design-process/research-phase/", "title": "www.uxdt.nic.in - Research Phase", "section": "Overview", "text": "The research phase is a critical step in the UX design process, laying the foundation for creating user-centric platforms. It involves gathering insights into user needs, behaviors, and pain points to inform design decisions. This phase ensures the platform is aligned with real-world expectations and delivers meaningful solutions to users. Why is Research Important? Research helps design teams: - Understand Users: Gain a deep understanding of target users’ goals, challenges, and preferences. - Identify Problems: Uncover pain points and gaps in existing systems. - Validate Assumptions: Replace assumptions with data-driven insights. - Guide Design Decisions: Provide a strong foundation for creating intuitive and efficient solutions. Key Steps in the Research Phase 1. Define Research Objectives - Clearly outline what you want to learn and how the insights will be used. - Focus on understanding user demographics, behaviors, goals, and frustrations. Example Objectives: - Understand why users abandon a subsidy application portal midway. - Identify features users value most in a government mobile app. 2. Identify Research Methods Choose research methods based on project scope, timelines, and resources. Common methods include: - User Interviews: - Conduct one-on-one interviews to gather qualitative insights about user needs, preferences, and experiences. - Ask open-ended questions like: - “What challenges do you face when accessing government services online?” - “What features would make this platform more useful for you?” - Surveys and Questionnaires: - Use online surveys to collect feedback from a larger audience. - Focus on specific metrics such as ease of use, accessibility, or satisfaction levels. - Observation and Contextual Inquiry: - Observe users interacting with existing systems to identify usability issues. - Conduct contextual inquiries by visiting users’ environments (e.g., rural areas with limited internet access). - Analytics Review: - Analyze data from existing platforms to identify usage patterns, drop-off points, and popular features. - Competitive Analysis: - Study similar platforms to identify strengths, weaknesses, and best practices. - Compare user flows, features, and design elements. 3. Develop User Personas - Create personas that represent the characteristics, needs, and goals of different user groups. - Include details like: - Demographics: Age, location, education. - Goals: What they want to achieve on the platform. - Pain Points: Challenges they face while using digital services. Example Persona: - Name: Ravi Kumar - Age: 45 - Goal: Apply for agricultural subsidies. - Pain Point: Difficulty navigating forms on a mobile device. 4. Map User Journeys - Visualize the steps users take to achieve their goals on the platform. - Identify touchpoints, pain points, and opportunities for improvement. Example Journey: - User searches for subsidy eligibility criteria. - User downloads the application form. - User struggles to upload required documents due to unclear instructions. - User abandons the process. 5. Organize and Analyze Findings - Compile research data into actionable insights. - Use affinity mapping or other techniques to group common themes and issues. Key Outputs:", "category": "civic", "last_verified": "2026-10-25", "status": "verified", "quality_score": 0.92, "source_confidence": "official", "verification_method": "automated_official_source", "retrieval_tags": ["about", "apply", "documents", "eligibility", "help", "info", "portal", "process", "service", "wwwuxdtnicin"], "language": "en"}
{"id": "civic_guidelinesindiagovin_8c5a963f_01", "source_url": "https://guidelines.india.gov.in/security-guidelines-and-attributes/", "title": "3. Security: Guidelines and Attributes | Guidelines for Indian Government Websites and apps (GIGW) | India", "section": "Overview", "text": "GIGW 3.0 Security Protecting web resources from unauthorised use, access, changes, destruction, or disruption is generally termed as “Website Security” or “Secured Website”. Sometimes web resources become unavailable due to denial-of-service attacks or display modified information on the webpages. Millions of passwords, email addresses and credit card details have been leaked into the public domain exposing web users to both personal embarrassment and financial risks. The purpose of Website Security is to prevent such risks. Website Security requires vigilance in all aspects starting from design, coding and implementation to testing and deployment. Organisations should implement appropriate security majors, defences and countermeasures to protect web resources against malfunctioning, phishing, cyber-crimes or cyberattacks to avoid data loss of the organisations or users. The government organisation will ensure and monitor that the host service provider and the developer adhere to the industry best security practices and guidelines such as ISO 27001, OWASP ASVS, OWASP Top 10 vulnerabilities and CIS benchmarks as per the prevailing security policy. Following guidelines are to secure web resources & associated infrastructure: 5.3.1 Statement: Website, web application, web portal or mobile app have been Security Audited and an Audit Clearance certificate has been issued by NIC/ STQC/ STQC empanelled laboratory/CERT-In empanelled laboratory before hosting in production environment. Benefits : The goal of securing a website, web application, web portal or mobile app is to maintain the confidentiality, integrity and availability of information and services. This goal is accomplished through the implementation of best security practices in design, development and deployment. Attacks could cause both personal embarrassment and financial risks. Government organisation action: It should be ensured that the website, web application, web portal or mobile app don’t have any security risks as identified by the latest OWASP Top 10 vulnerability list. The design and development agency or the developers should follow industry best practices such as OWASP ASVS and OWASP MAVS. Developer Action : Securing critical web resources is more important than ever as the focus of attackers has steadily moved towards the application layer and they are exploiting the weaknesses in the code. - Securing Code: - Ensure that all Websites, Web Applications, Web Portals or Mobile Apps and their respective Content Management System (CMS), 3rd party plugins, codes, etc. are updated to the latest versions. Note: Every day, there are countless websites compromised due to outdated software. Potential hackers and bots are scanning sites to attack. Updates are vital to the health and security of the website. If the site’s software or applications are not up-to-date, the site is not secure. Take all software and plugin update requests seriously. Updates often contain security enhancements and vulnerability repairs. Check the site for updates or add an update notification plugin. Some platforms allow automatic updates, which is another option to ensure website security. The longer you wait, the less secure the site will be. Updating the website and its", "category": "civic", "last_verified": "2026-10-25", "status": "verified", "quality_score": 0.92, "source_confidence": "official", "verification_method": "automated_official_source", "retrieval_tags": ["guideline", "guidelinesindiagovin", "info", "portal", "service"], "language": "en"}
{"id": "civic_haryanaitgovin_21d16913_01", "source_url": "https://haryanait.gov.in/s3waas/", "title": "S3waas |  Department of Information Technology Electronics & Communication, Haryana | India", "section": "Overview", "text": "S3waas S3WaaS (Secure, Scalable, and Accessible Websites as a Service) is a cloud-based platform designed to develop secure, scalable, and accessible websites for government organizations. It enables government entities to easily create, customize, and manage website content through a variety of standardized templates, thereby ensuring a strong and consistent online presence. The Government has developed 52 websites of Haryana for various Departments, Boards, and Corporations, along with 22 District websites, on the Secure, Scalable, and Sugamya Website as a Service (S3WaaS) platform of NIC. List of websites developed on the S3WaaS platform | Sr. No. | Department / Board | URL | | 1 | Government of Haryana | haryana.gov.in | | 2 | Electronics and Information Technology Department, Haryana | haryanait.gov.in | | 3 | Directorate of Micro, Small and Medium Enterprises | msme.haryana.gov.in | | 4 | Haryana Gau Seva Aayog | hargauseva.gov.in | | 5 | Women and Child Development Department | wcdhry.gov.in | | 6 | ESI Department | hryesi.gov.in | | 7 | Department of Economic and Statistical Analysis | esaharyana.gov.in | | 8 | State Council of Educational Research and Training | scertharyana.gov.in | | 9 | Treasuries & Accounts Department | hrtreasuries.gov.in | | 10 | Department of Architecture, Haryana | architecturehry.gov.in | | 11 | Revenue and Disaster Management Department | revenueharyana.gov.in | | 12 | Department of New and Renewable Energy, Haryana | hareda.gov.in | | 13 | State Finance Commission | sfc.haryana.gov.in | | 14 | ENVIS Hub, Environment and Climate Change Department, Haryana | envis.haryana.gov.in | | 15 | School Education (Primary) | harprathmik.gov.in | | 16 | School Education Department | schooleducationharyana.gov.in | | 17 | State Vigilance Bureau | acb.haryana.gov.in | | 18 | Supplies and Disposal Department | dsndharyana.gov.in | | 19 | State Wetland Authority, Haryana | swa.haryana.gov.in | | 20 | Haryana Forest Department | haryanaforest.gov.in | | 21 | Environment and Climate Change Department, Haryana | hrccc.harenvironment.gov.in | | 22 | Haryana State Biodiversity Board | sbb.haryanaforest.gov.in | | 23 | Swarna Jayanti Environment Training Institute, Manesar, Gurugram | sjeti.haryana.gov.in | | 24 | Social Justice and Empowerment Department | socialjusticehry.gov.in | | 25 | Haryana Right to Service Commission | haryana-rtsc.gov.in | | 26 | Food, Civil Supplies and Consumer Affairs Department, Haryana | haryanafood.gov.in | | 27 | Raj Bhavan, Haryana | haryanarajbhavan.gov.in | | 28 | Civil Aviation Department | haraviation.gov.in | | 29 | Finance Department, Haryana | finhry.gov.in | | 30 | Haryana Forest Development Corporation Limited | hfdc.gov.in | | 31 | State Police Complaint Authority, Haryana | spcahry.nic.in | | 32 | Haryana State Legal Services Authority | hslsa.gov.in | | 33 | CAMPA, Haryana | campa.haryanaforest.gov.in | | 34 | AB-HHPA State Health Authority | ayushmanbharat.haryana.gov.in | | 35 | SEWA Department, Haryana | sewa.haryana.gov.in | | 36 | Archives Department, Haryana | haryanaarchives.gov.in |", "category": "civic", "last_verified": "2026-10-25", "status": "verified", "quality_score": 0.92, "source_confidence": "official", "verification_method": "automated_official_source", "retrieval_tags": ["haryanaitgovin", "info", "service"], "language": "en"}
{"id": "civic_haryanaitgovin_6f2bd7cb_01", "source_url": "https://haryanait.gov.in/about-department/vision-mission/", "title": "Vision & Mission |  Department of Information Technology Electronics & Communication, Haryana | India", "section": "Overview", "text": "Vision & Mission VISION The Government of Haryana recognizes the important role of Information and Communication Technologies (ICT) in delivery of citizen services and governance processes in an efficient and transparent manner. To facilitate planning, designing, implementing and managing a wide range of IT initiatives at department/ organization levels, the State Government of Haryana has aligned its vision to focus on Digital India Program’s three important Vision areas/ Objectives and nine pillars: Objectives - Digital Infrastructure as a Utility to Every Citizen - Governance & Services on Demand - Digital Empowerment of Citizens Nine Pillars of Digital India Program: - Broadband Highways - SWAN, NOFN, NKN, Rural/Urban Wi-Fi, Cloud Computing, NII (National Information Infra) - Universal Access to Mobile Connectivity – NOFN, CCIP Policy - NOFN, CCIP Policy - Public Internet Access Programme - CSC, Post Office, Mass IT Literacy NOFN - e-Governance – Reforming Government through Technology - e-Office, ERP, HRMS, IVRS, Aadhaar based e-KYC Services, IT Cadre - E-Kranti – Electronic delivery of services - 250+ e-Services (G2C, B2C, B2B, G2B, etc), Poerals, Apps (Portals, Mobile), ISMO - Information for All - MyGov Haryana, e-Services, State Portals, Websites, Cyber Security Policy etc. - Electronics Manufacturing - Incubation Centres, IoT, STPI, IIIT, ITIR etc. - IT for Jobs - NDLM, Digital Literacy, Skill Development, Rural BPO, IT / ESDM/ Start-up Policy etc. - Early Harvest Programmes - RAS, Digital Locker, e-Sign, IVRS etc. MISSION To promote e-Governance for empowering citizens, promoting the inclusive and sustainable growth of the Electronics, IT & ITeS industries, enhancing State Government of Haryana’s role in Internet Governance, adopting a multipronged approach that includes development of human resources, promoting R&D and innovation, enhancing efficiency through digital services and ensuring a secure cyber space. The mission of the State is: - e-Government: To position Haryana as the preferred IT/ITES investment destination in India. - e-Industry: Promotion of electronics hardware manufacturing and IT-ITeS industry - e-Innovation / R&D: Implementation of R&D Framework – Enabling creation of Innovation/ R&D Infrastructure in emerging areas of ICT&E/Establishment of mechanism for R&D translation - e-Learning: Providing support for development of e-Skills and Knowledge network - e-Security: Securing State’s cyber space - e-Inclusion: Promoting the use of ICT for more inclusive growth - Internet Governance: Enhancing State’s role in Global Platforms of Internet Governance.", "category": "civic", "last_verified": "2026-10-25", "status": "verified", "quality_score": 0.92, "source_confidence": "official", "verification_method": "automated_official_source", "retrieval_tags": ["citizen", "haryanaitgovin", "info", "portal", "process", "service"], "language": "en"}
