{"id": "civic_taxguruin_932a67f2_01", "source_url": "https://taxguru.in/goods-and-service-tax/hsn-sac-code-mandatory-invoice-form-gstr-1.html", "title": "HSN & SAC Code Mandatory in Invoice & Form GSTR-1", "section": "Overview", "text": "HSN & SAC Code Are Mandatory In Invoice And Form GSTR-1 W.E.F.01.04.2021 Brief Notes on mandatory mentioning of HSN & SAC Code in Invoice and Form GSTr-1 as per amendments to GST Law, 2017 w.e.f.01.04.2021. Dear Professional Colleagues, Good Day to you. I have gone through recent changes about mandatory to mention HSN & SAC Code in Tax Invoice and furnishing inward & outward details at box no 12 of GSTR-1 w.e.f.01.04.2021. Some of the professionals are having confusion about mentioning of HSN & SAC code in Tax Invoice and Furnishing inward & outward details in GSTR-1. How many Digit of HSN & SAC Codes are to mention in the Tax invoice & GSTR-1 of GST Law, 2017. Is there any threshold limit to mention HSN &SAC Codes for supply of Goods and Services from 01.04.2021? So, here with I am trying to clarify at my best of Knowledge. Please refer and provide your suggestions to my mail i.d. [email protected] or what’s app No.9848099490. As per the below mentioned notifications CBIC has clarified who are liable to mentioned HSN & SAC Code on Tax Invoice and Form GSTR-1 w.e.f..1.4.2021, | S.NO. | Notification No. | Year | Date of Issue. | |---|---|---|---| | 1 | Notification No. 12/2027- Central Tax | 2017 | 28.06.2017 | | 2 | Notification No. 78/2020- Central Tax | 2020 | 15.10.2020 | | 3 | Notification No. 82/2020- Central Tax | 2020 | 10.11.2020 | | 4 | Notification No. 90/2020- Central Tax | 2020 | 01.12.2020 | | 5 | Notification No. 12/2021- Central Tax | 2021 | 01.05.2021 | On the recommendations of the GST Council the Government of India , has given the above notification from time to time through CBIC. The CBIC has mentioned in such Notifications that they have bifurcated into 2 types taxpayers based on Aggregate Turnover of Previous Financial Year. As per Notification No.82/2020 dated.10-11-2020, In FORM GSTR-1, in the Instructions, after serial number 17, the following instruction shall be inserted, namely:- “18. It will be mandatory to specify the number of digits of HSN code for goods or services that a class of registered persons shall be required to mention as may be specified in the notification issued from time to time under proviso to rule 46 of the said rules. As per Notification No.12/2021 –Central Tax , dt.1.5.2021 CBIC has given notification for extension the date to furnish the details as per amendment to GSTR-1 for the month of 4/2021 till 26.05.2021. Catergory-1 “ The tax payers is having Aggregate Turnover is more than Rs.5 Cr in the previous Financial Year and Category -2” The tax payers is having Aggregate Turnover is below Rs.5 Cr in the previous Financial Year. Category-1: They have to mention HSN Code for supply of Goods to B2B ,B2C and unregistered dealers as below Table: (Aggregate Turnover More Than", "category": "civic", "last_verified": "2026-10-25", "status": "verified", "quality_score": 0.92, "source_confidence": "official", "verification_method": "automated_official_source", "retrieval_tags": ["about", "rules", "service", "taxguruin"], "language": "en"}
{"id": "civic_taxguruin_c8279fe8_01", "source_url": "https://taxguru.in/goods-and-service-tax/portal-filing-barrier-telangana-hc-physical-gst-registration-revocation-application.html", "title": "taxguru.in - Portal Filing Barrier: Telangana HC Allows Physical GST Registration Revocation Application October 25, 2026", "section": "Overview", "text": "Bhumaiah Electricals and Civil Works Vs Deputy State Tax Office (Telangana High Court) Summary: The Telangana High Court disposed of the writ petition filed by Bhumaiah Electricals and Civil Works by granting an opportunity to apply for revocation of cancellation of GST registration. The petitioner’s registration, bearing GSTIN 36ERRPB7949M1ZU, had been cancelled through Form GST REG-19 dated 22 March 2025 for non-filing of returns for six consecutive months. Its appeal against the cancellation was dismissed as time-barred. The petitioner submitted that heavy business losses following the Covid-19 pandemic prevented continuation of its business and that its accountant failed to file returns after October 2023. According to the petitioner, the proprietor had relied entirely on the accountant for monthly return filing, was unaware of the show cause notice, and had not intentionally delayed compliance. Since the GST portal prevented submission of a revocation application beyond the prescribed time limit, the petitioner sought permission for manual filing. The State confirmed that six months’ non-filing was the apparent reason for cancellation. Having regard to these circumstances, the Court granted liberty to approach the competent authority within two weeks from the date of the order and submit the application online, or in physical form if technical reasons prevented online acceptance. The authority was directed to entertain the application and decide it in accordance with law within three weeks thereafter. The relief was an opportunity to seek revocation; the Court did not itself restore the registration. The petition was disposed of without costs, and pending miscellaneous applications were closed. FULL TEXT OF THE JUDGMENT/ORDER OF TELANGANA HIGH COURT Heard Mr. K.P. Amarnath Reddy, learned counsel for the petitioner and Mr. Swaroop Oorilla, learned Special Government Pleader for State Tax appearing for the respondents. 2. The Goods and Services Tax (GST) registration certificate of the petitioner bearing No.36ERRPB7949M1ZU was cancelled vide impugned order in Form GST REG-19 dated 22.03.2025 for non-tiling of returns for a consecutive period of six months. The petitioner preferred a time-barred appeal against the order of cancellation of GST registration. which has been dismissed on the ground of delay. Thereafter, the petitioner has filed the instant Writ Petition for revocation of cancellation of GST registration. 3. Learned counsel for the petitioner submits that aftermath Covid-19 pandemic, petitioner suffered heavy losses in the business and could not continue its business and its accountant failed to file returns after October, 2023. It is submitted that non-filing of returns was for the reason that the proprietor of the petitioner has completely relied upon the Accountant for filing of the monthly returns and he was also unaware of the show cause notice and there was no intentional delay. Though the petitioner has sought to file an application for revocation of cancellation of GST registration, the GST portal did not permit the petitioner as being beyond the time limit prescribed for submission. Therefore, the petitioner prays that", "category": "civic", "last_verified": "2026-10-25", "status": "verified", "quality_score": 0.92, "source_confidence": "official", "verification_method": "automated_official_source", "retrieval_tags": ["apply", "manual", "portal", "registration", "service", "taxguruin"], "language": "en"}
{"id": "civic_taxguruin_3fe7c2cb_01", "source_url": "https://taxguru.in/goods-and-service-tax/telangana-hc-fresh-manual-gst-registration-revocation-application.html", "title": "Telangana HC Allows Fresh Manual GST Registration Revocation Application", "section": "Overview", "text": "Jaycon Homes Private Limited Vs Deputy State Tax Officer (Telangana High Court) Telangana High Court Permits Fresh Application for Revocation of GST Registration Cancellation Despite Earlier Rejection Summary: The Telangana High Court, in M/s. Jaycon Homes Private Limited Vs Deputy State Tax Officer & Others, considered a writ petition arising from the rejection of an application for revocation of cancellation of GST registration. The petitioner had applied for revocation on 25.03.2025 against the cancellation order dated 17.03.2025 issued in Form GST REG-19. A show cause notice was subsequently issued on 28.04.2026, more than one year after the revocation application. Since no reply was filed, the competent authority rejected the application on 12.05.2026. By then, the period available for filing an appeal had also expired. The petitioner submitted that the show cause notice had been uploaded on the GST portal without separate intimation and that its tax consultant had not been regularly monitoring the portal. Consequently, the petitioner was unable to respond within the required period. It sought another opportunity either to submit a reply to the earlier notice or to make a fresh revocation application. The State Tax Department’s counsel indicated that the petitioner could be permitted to submit a fresh application within a stipulated period for consideration in accordance with law.", "category": "civic", "last_verified": "2026-10-25", "status": "verified", "quality_score": 0.92, "source_confidence": "official", "verification_method": "automated_official_source", "retrieval_tags": ["portal", "registration", "taxguruin"], "language": "en"}
{"id": "civic_taxguruin_01a846fb_01", "source_url": "https://taxguru.in/service-tax/hospital-food-supply-attracts-catering-service-tax-extended-demand-fails-cestat-bangalore.html", "title": "taxguru.in - Service Tax Hospital Food Supply Attracts Catering Service Tax; Extended Demand Fails: CESTAT Bangalore", "section": "Overview", "text": "Annapoorna Health Foods Vs Commissioner of Central Excise and Service Tax (CESTAT Bangalore) Summary: CESTAT Bangalore partly allowed the appeal of Annapoorna Health Foods concerning service tax on food supplied to patients, their relatives, doctors and other staff of Narayana Hridayalaya Pvt. Ltd. The dispute covered 1 March 2006 to 30 September 2010, and the show cause notice was issued on 21 April 2011. The appellant contended that its activity constituted sale of food on which VAT had been paid, rather than outdoor catering service. It relied on its VAT composition returns, the agreement dated 2 December 2005 and a statement that hospital personnel collected food from its counter and distributed it to patients. It also invoked Circular No. 96/7/2007-ST dated 23 August 2007 and Notification No. 12/2003-ST dated 20 June 2003 concerning exclusion of the value of goods sold. The Revenue argued that the arrangement involved institutional catering from approximately 1,000 square feet of space provided by the hospital. According to its submissions, food was prepared against ward requirements, customised for patients and billed monthly to the hospital, which controlled operating hours, menus, cleanliness and service quality. The Revenue also referred to the hospital’s accounts and canteen sales records, including patient-food purchases of ₹52,96,129 from the appellant and total canteen sales of ₹22,80,195 for March 2006. It submitted that VAT on the goods component did not preclude service tax on the service component and that the applicable abatements and deductions had already been granted. On limitation, it relied on the appellant’s failure to obtain service tax registration, file ST-3 returns or disclose the activity to the service tax authorities. The Tribunal held that the activity fell within Sections 65(24), 65(76a) and 65(105)(zzt) of the Finance Act, 1994. The definition covered catering from premises provided by the service recipient, and the appellant supplied food from the hospital premises. Payment of VAT established taxation of the goods component but did not discharge service tax on the services rendered. Since statutory abatements and deductions had already been allowed, the confirmed demand related to the service portion. The Tribunal further noted that Notification No. 21/2004-ST, which had exempted specified outdoor catering within medical establishments, was rescinded by Notification No. 2/2006-ST with effect from 1 March 2006. It distinguished the appellant’s authorities by reference to the facts and provisions applicable during the disputed period. However, the Tribunal rejected the extended-period demand and penalties because the entire demand was based on records maintained by the appellant. Relying on Anand Nishikawa, Pushpam Pharmaceutical and Cosmic Di Chemicals as cited in the order, it held that mere failure to declare facts or an omission did not establish deliberate suppression with intent to evade tax. It also referred to the Karnataka High Court’s decision in Motor World concerning relief from penalties under the then-existing Section 80. The appeal was therefore partly allowed: service tax was confirmed only for", "category": "civic", "last_verified": "2026-10-25", "status": "verified", "quality_score": 0.92, "source_confidence": "official", "verification_method": "automated_official_source", "retrieval_tags": ["acts", "registration", "service", "taxguruin"], "language": "en"}
{"id": "civic_taxguruin_c2eb6594_01", "source_url": "https://taxguru.in/goods-and-service-tax/cbic-notifies-new-rules-inward-outward-supplies-gst-returns-new-form-gstr-2b.html", "title": "taxguru.in - Notification No. 82/2020- Central Tax", "section": "Overview", "text": "CBIC has vide Notification No. 82/2020–Central Tax dated 10th November, 2020 amended/substituted and inserted GST Rules as follows:- 1. Substituted rule 59 related to Form and manner of furnishing details of outward supplies with effect from the 1st day of January, 2021 2. Substituted rule 60 related to Form and manner of ascertaining details of inward supplies with effect from the 1st day of January, 2021. 3. Inserted Sub Rule (6) to rule 61 with effect from 10th November, 2020 which related to Filing of GSTR 3B Return. 4. Substituted rule 61 related to Form and manner of furnishing of return with effect from the 1st day of January, 2021. 5. Inserted Rule 61A related to Manner of opting for furnishing quarterly return with effect from 10th November, 2020. 6. Amended Rule 62 related to Form and manner of submission of statement and return, with effect from 10th November, 2020. 7. Inserted new Instruction to filing of FORM GSTR-1, in the Instructions which related mentioning of HSN code . 8. Notified new FORM-2B – Auto-drafted ITC Statement- under 60(7). MINISTRY OF FINANCE (Department of Revenue) (CENTRAL BOARD OF INDIRECT TAXES AND CUSTOMS) NOTIFICATION New Delhi, the 10th November, 2020 Notification No. 82/2020–Central Tax G.S.R. 698(E).—In exercise of the powers conferred by section 164 of the Central Goods and Services Tax Act, 2017 (12 of 2017), the Central Government, on recommendations of the Council, hereby makes the following rules further to amend the Central Goods and Services Tax Rules, 2017, namely: – 1. Short title and commencement. – (1) These rules may be called the Central Goods and Services Tax (Thirteenth Amendment) Rules, 2020. (2) Save as otherwise provided in these rules, they shall come into force on the date of their publication in the Official Gazette. 2. In the Central Goods and Services Tax Rules, 2017 (hereafter in this notification referred to as the said rules), for rule 59, the following rule shall be substituted with effect from the 1st day of January, 2021 namely: – “59. Form and manner of furnishing details of outward supplies.- (1) Every registered person, other than a person referred to in section 14 of the Integrated Goods and Services Tax Act, 2017 (13 of 2017), required to furnish the details of outward supplies of goods or services or both under section 37, shall furnish such details in FORM GSTR-1 for the month or the quarter, as the case may be, electronically through the common portal, either directly or through a Facilitation Centre as may be notified by the Commissioner. (2) The registered persons required to furnish return for every quarter under proviso to sub-section (1) of section 39 may furnish the details of such outward supplies of goods or services or both to a registered person, as he may consider necessary, for the first and second months of a quarter, up to a cumulative", "category": "civic", "last_verified": "2026-10-25", "status": "verified", "quality_score": 0.92, "source_confidence": "official", "verification_method": "automated_official_source", "retrieval_tags": ["portal", "rules", "service", "taxguruin"], "language": "en"}
{"id": "civic_seconizeco_2173468c_01", "source_url": "https://seconize.co/uncategorized/faqs-on-cybersecurity-and-cyber-resilience-framework/", "title": "FAQs on Cybersecurity and Cyber Resilience Framework (CSCRF) for SEBI REs and Framework for Adoption of Cloud Services by SEBI REs - Seconize", "section": "Overview", "text": "FAQs on Cybersecurity and Cyber Resilience Framework (CSCRF) for SEBI REs and Framework for Adoption of Cloud Services by SEBI REs Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) on Cybersecurity and Cyber Resilience Framework (CSCRF) for SEBI REs and Framework for Adoption of Cloud Services by SEBI REs These FAQs aim to address the most common queries/ questions SEBI REs have about the CSCRF and Framework for Adoption of Cloud Services by SEBI REs in a simple and understandable manner. FAQs have been consolidated into following categories: - Governance and CISO related guidelines - Thresholds for REs’ categorization - Asset Inventory and Classification of Critical/ Non-critical systems - VAPT and Patch Management - Cyber Audit and Timelines - Cyber Capability Index (CCI) - Software Bill of Materials (SBOM) - Outsourcing related guidelines - Cloud Service Providers (CSPs) and Hosted Services - COTS product testing - Log Management, Data Security, and other Protect guidelines - ISO 27001 certification - Security Operations Centre (SOC) and Market-SOC (M-SOC) - Threat Intelligence - DC-DR Drills - Response and Recovery - Classification and Handling of Cybersecurity Incidents Governance and CISO related guidelines - Are banks required to submit a certificate of compliance to SEBI? What specific aspects of the CSCRF or RBI cybersecurity framework need to be covered in the certificate? Answer: Banker to an Issue (BTI) and SCSBs shall submit a certificate of compliance to SEBI on the cybersecurity guidelines issued by RBI. Banks which have taken other SEBI registrations, shall be required to comply with CSCRF (as applicable). However, as clarified in Q.29, the controls shall apply only to IT infrastructure, network, application, software, etc. being used for SEBI RE related activities. - As per RBI’s directions, Bank’s CISO are reporting to their Executive Director (ED) in charge of Risk function. However, CSCRF mandates CISOs’ of MIIs and Qualified REs to report directly to the MD/ CEO of their organisation. Can this specific exemption be given to such SEBI REs (operating also as Banks under RBI regulations) to make it uniform? Answer: As per CSCRF guidelines, the level, grade, and standing of CISO shall be at least equivalent to CTO/ CIO for MIIs and Qualified REs. Accordingly, the reporting of CISO to ED or MD/ CEO as per organisational structure shall be deemed compliant. - Can a group-level CISO be designated as the effective CISO for multiple entities within the same group, especially for small-size, QRE, and mid-size REs? Answer: Yes, group level CISO can be designated as the effective CISO for multiple entities within the same group. - CSCRF requires REs to mandatorily have a CISO. Is it acceptable for REs to hire a part-time CISO who simultaneously holds CISO position at multiple REs? Answer: For the ease of compliance without any additional burden, REs are allowed to onboard a remote CISO, provided the personnel is only dedicated to one specific organisation and not managing", "category": "civic", "last_verified": "2026-10-25", "status": "verified", "quality_score": 0.92, "source_confidence": "official", "verification_method": "automated_official_source", "retrieval_tags": ["about", "apply", "faq", "frequently", "guideline", "registration", "seconizeco", "service"], "language": "en"}
